Guidelines for law enforcement information requests
Last updated April 9, 2024
The information provided in this section is intended for use by U.S. law enforcement when seeking information from Thumbtack, Inc. (“Thumbtack”). We may change these guidelines at any time. Private parties, including civil litigants and criminal defendants, should consult our guidelines for third party information requests.
For all requests for disclosure of information maintained on Thumbtack's platform, including information sought in connection with an official law enforcement investigation, Thumbtack requires that the appropriate form of legal process be issued in accordance with applicable federal state, and local laws, as well as applicable rules and regulations. We generally require a valid search warrant prior to disclosing the contents of communications between or among Consumers and Service Professionals.
Business and account records. We store, maintain, use, and disseminate information as described in our Terms of Use and Privacy Policy. This includes any information that is provided when Consumers or Service Professionals sign up and use the service, as well as information created and maintained during the ordinary course of using our service. This information may include, but is not limited to, account name(s), Thumbtack user identification number(s), business name(s), phone number(s), email address(es), account start and end date, registration IP address, account status, user rating, payment method, payment history, quote history including dates, times, and locations, customer service communications, uploaded photographs, and registered current and historical address(es).
Preservation of records. Thumbtack receives formal requests for preservation of records through its secure law enforcement portal. We will make commercially reasonable efforts to preserve records connected with an official governmental investigation for a minimum of 90 days. Law enforcement may extend a preservation request for additional days. We may not maintain preserved materials beyond the initial 90 days unless we receive an extension request or formal legal process.
Subpoena format and required information. We review each subpoena and other forms of legal process for facial and substantive validity. Thumbtack may object to requests that are not facially or substantively valid, including but not limited to any requests that are overly broad or vague.
In order to make sure your subpoena or other legal process is valid, we ask for the following:
A sufficiently narrow request with a defined timeframe and identifying the information sought with particularity;
A specific person, entity, or reference (i.e., you must uniquely identify a user, phone number, service provider, credit card number, or some type of other specific characteristic to limit the scope of the request);
Requester's contact information (i.e., name, title, department, government email address, and phone number);
The reason for your request.
Service of legal documents: Law enforcement agencies can submit a data or preservation request by creating an account and signing into our secure law enforcement portal.
Please note that we will not respond to any requests submitted via fax, phone, email or any requests made by unauthorized persons. If submitted via mail, law enforcement officials should expect longer response times.
Legal process may also be served at our San Francisco Headquarters:
Thumbtack Inc. Attn: Legal Department 415 Natoma St, Suite 1300 San Francisco, CA 94103
Our acceptance of legal process does not waive any legal objections Thumbtack may have and may raise in response to the request.
Notification to user. If we are required by law to disclose user information, it is our policy to use commercially reasonable efforts to notify the user unless (a) we believe in our sole discretion that providing notice could create a risk of injury or death, or that harm or fraud could be directed to Thumbtack or users; or (b) we are precluded from providing notice by law. This user notice will be sent via email to the email address listed on the account. Law enforcement officials seeking non-disclosure of legal process should obtain an appropriate court order establishing that notice is prohibited before serving legal process on Thumbtack or should provide enough details of their investigation to us that will enable us to determine whether a request falls into an exception to our policy.
Emergency and exigent information requests. Consistent with our privacy policy, we may disclose information to law enforcement in response to a valid emergency request.
Visit the secure law enforcement portal to make an emergency request.
All requests will be reviewed on a case-by-case basis. In the request, please include the following information:
The person(s) who is in danger of death or serious injury, if any;
Specific threat or exigency;
Specific user information (e.g., name, phone number, email etc.);
Information requested (e.g., address, recent quote history, messaging history);
Signature, title, ID#, and Agency of requesting agent;
Relevant legal authority for the emergency request (e.g., reference to statute or rule of criminal procedure);
Any other pertinent information related to the request
Scenarios that we would deem to be emergency cases include (but are not limited to):
current kidnapping or missing person situation;
active sexual assault, including but not limited to child sexual abuse;
school shooting threats when serious and imminent;
acts of terrorism;
an imminent threat to life.
If you have any questions, please contact us through the Quick Questions option in Kodex.
